/-
# A finitely generated non-prosoluble profinite CN-group (Kourovka Notebook Problem 20.90)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 20.90 (P. Shumyatsky), marked solved in the 21st issue: a profinite group
in which all centralizers of non-trivial elements are pronilpotent is called a CN-group. Find an
example of a finitely generated infinite profinite CN-group which is not prosoluble.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Nilpotent
public import Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.Group.Subgroup

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ProfiniteCNGroupNotProsolubleKourovka20Problem90Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 20.90 (P. Shumyatsky). UnsolvedMath record
UM-00701748 (`KOU-20.90`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Profinite groups are compact Hausdorff totally disconnected topological groups in `Type`; finitely
generated means topologically: the closure of the subgroup generated by a finite set is everything.
A subgroup is pronilpotent when its image in `G / N` is nilpotent for every open normal `N` (for a
closed subgroup, an inverse limit of finite nilpotent groups), and `G` is prosoluble when every `G /
N` with `N` open normal is soluble. "Find an example" is stated as existence.
-/

/-- **Pronilpotent subgroup.** A subgroup $H$ of a group $G$ carrying a topology is pronilpotent
when, for every open normal subgroup $N$ of $G$, the image of $H$ in the quotient $G/N$ is
nilpotent.

For a closed subgroup of a profinite group this says that $H$ is an inverse limit of finite
nilpotent groups, since the subgroups $H \cap N$ are cofinal among the open normal subgroups of
$H$. -/
def IsPronilpotent {G : Type} [Group G] [TopologicalSpace G] (H : Subgroup G) : Prop :=
  ∀ (N : Subgroup G) [N.Normal], IsOpen (N : Set G) →
    Group.IsNilpotent (H.map (QuotientGroup.mk' N))

/-- **Problem of a finitely generated non-prosoluble profinite CN-group (Kourovka 20.90).** The
statement that there is an infinite profinite group $G$, that is, a compact, Hausdorff, totally
disconnected topological group, such that $G$ is topologically finitely generated (the closure of
the subgroup generated by some finite set is $G$), the centralizer of every non-trivial element of
$G$ is pronilpotent (so $G$ is a CN-group), and $G$ is not prosoluble: for some open normal
subgroup $N$ of $G$, the quotient $G/N$ is not soluble. -/
def existsFGInfiniteProfiniteCNGroupNotProsoluble : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G) (_ : TopologicalSpace G) (_ : IsTopologicalGroup G)
    (_ : CompactSpace G) (_ : T2Space G) (_ : TotallyDisconnectedSpace G),
    Infinite G ∧ (∃ s : Finset G, (Subgroup.closure (s : Set G)).topologicalClosure = ⊤) ∧
    (∀ g : G, g ≠ 1 → IsPronilpotent (Subgroup.centralizer {g})) ∧
    ¬ ∀ (N : Subgroup G) [N.Normal], IsOpen (N : Set G) → Group.IsSolvable (G ⧸ N)

/--
**A finitely generated non-prosoluble profinite CN-group exists.** There is an infinite profinite
group $G$, that is, a compact, Hausdorff, totally disconnected topological group, such that $G$ is
topologically finitely generated (the closure of the subgroup generated by some finite set is $G$),
the centralizer of every non-trivial element of $G$ is pronilpotent, and $G$ is not prosoluble: for
some open normal subgroup $N$ of $G$, the quotient $G/N$ is not soluble. This answers Kourovka
Problem 20.90.

Kinyon and Urban (Section 3.35) give an infinite two-generated closed subgroup of
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{F}_4[[t]])$ that maps onto $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{F}_4) \cong A_5$ and in
which every non-identity element has abelian, hence pronilpotent, centralizer.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov
(eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49
(2026), Problem 20.90 (P. Shumyatsky); resolution: M. Kinyon and J. Urban, Results on problems from
the Kourovka Notebook, preprint (September 2026), Kourovka Notebook site, Section 3.35: a
two-generated closed subgroup G of SL₂(F₄[[t]]) that is infinite, maps onto SL₂(F₄) ≅ A₅ by
reduction modulo t (so it is not prosoluble), and in which the centralizer of every non-identity
element is abelian; an AI solution confirmed by P. Shumyatsky, Kourovka Notebook (21st issue),
Problem 20.90: An example is found,
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/kourovka-mathematics.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted existsFGInfiniteProfiniteCNGroupNotProsoluble_holds :
    existsFGInfiniteProfiniteCNGroupNotProsoluble

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ProfiniteCNGroupNotProsolubleKourovka20Problem90Wanted
